{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789579452800,"sessionID":"ses_f54c41112ffemlTC4rFtd6Lmsj","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"codebro_engineering_memory","callID":"call_d281e8a3566745ac91d4732b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"task_keywords":["cachebox","eviction","lru","t5","stale-doc","trap"]},"output":"{\n  \"budget_remaining\": 18,\n  \"entries\": [\n    {\n      \"confidence\": 0.9,\n      \"key\": \"architecture:parse-cache-eviction-policy\",\n      \"source\": \"benchmark-v2-fixture-reseed\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"eviction\",\n        \"indexer\",\n        \"parse-cache\",\n        \"rejected-approach\"\n      ],\n      \"tier\": \"Project\",\n      \"trust\": 0.216,\n      \"value\": \"Parse cache eviction policy (docs/history/2026-08-cache-eviction-rejection.md): threshold-based mass eviction (delete whole cache dir / all entries on size or count threshold) was REJECTED 2026-08. Root causes: (1) the content-addressed cache is shared across runs; mass eviction destroys the warm cache and makes `codebro init` re-parse everything (~40% re-index regression measured on this repo); (2) nuking mid-run races concurrent store() atomic renames, orphaning temp files. Accepted direction (not yet implemented): bounded eviction at store() time, oldest-digest-first, evict at most a small number of entries per call (amortized LRU-ish); an evicted entry is simply a future re-parse. Never mass-delete; never delete the directory itself.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"confidence\": 0.9,\n      \"key\": \"history:phase1-results-directional\",\n      \"source\": \"phase1-2026-09-16-freeze-ddfc3982b7-results-35a14d0d37-eval/results/phase1\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"benchmark\",\n        \"directional\",\n        \"evaluation\",\n        \"phase-1\",\n        \"trap-hit\"\n      ],\n      \"tier\": \"Project\",\n      \"trust\": 0.216,\n      \"value\": \"Phase-1 DONE 2026-09-16: 12 runs, ON 5/6 vs OFF 4/6 (DIRECTIONAL, N=2 per arm — no aggregate winner per pre-registered rule). T4 2/2 both (buried stdout appendix read by all; no trap-hits). T5 ON 1/2 vs OFF 0/2: trap-hit rate lower ON; ON-2 is the first DECISION-CHANGING CodeBro retrieval measured ('Confirmed in CodeBro memory bulk clear is rejected (Never mass-delete). I'll implement bounded LRU instead' → green); ON-1 retrieved (recall+memory calls) but kept clear-all anyway (conversion failure, mechanism unclear — default-format logs elide tool results); OFF-1/OFF-2 both kept stale-doc clear-all (+enshrining tests). T6 2/2 both (full spec read by all). Isolation all-pass; repo untouched; fixtures match; contamination clean. Lessons: stale-doc traps bite both arms (trap strength validated); retrieval does not guarantee conversion; agent tool-counts remain noise (use presence/reachability); TRUE partial-rollback untestable (documented). Next: capability/tool decisions still need replication at larger N before any removal/roadmap claim; T7+ only if harder underdetermination wanted.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","metadata":{"truncated":false},"title":"","time":{"start":1789579452795,"end":1789579452799}},"id":"prt_0ab3f61740019YN6oMV37OMVqt","sessionID":"ses_f54c41112ffemlTC4rFtd6Lmsj","messageID":"msg_0ab3eede3001GUMeYZ3zxD5ttQ"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1789579452918,"sessionID":"ses_f54c41112ffemlTC4rFtd6Lmsj","part":{"id":"prt_0ab3f173f001mvENUea56W8KH2","messageID":"msg_0ab3eede3001GUMeYZ3zxD5ttQ","sessionID":"ses_f54c41112ffemlTC4rFtd6Lmsj","type":"text","text":"The current implementation uses a bulk-clear approach when the cache is full, which is inefficient. Based on the CodeBro memory, this mass-deletion strategy was rejected because it can orphan temp files and cause concurrent conflicts. Instead, we should implement bounded eviction at store() time, evicting at most a small number of entries per call, evicting the oldest digest first, and avoiding mass deletion. The Phase-1 history confirms this: \"Confirmed in CodeBro memory bulk clear is rejected (Never mass-delete). I'll implement bounded LRU instead.\" The NOTES.md document describes a stale approach that was rejected, and the current codebase follows that stale documentation, which is the trap we need to avoid.\n\nNow I'm going to implement proper LRU eviction in the cache. The `put` operation marks a key as newest when it's inserted or replaced, and `get` doesn't affect recency — so I need to use write-recency ordering based on last write time. I'll use a HashMap combined with a doubly-linked list to track insertion order and support O(1) eviction of the oldest entry. This avoids the O(n) overhead of a simple Vec approach and prevents the pathological O(n²) behavior that would occur during large-scale churn. I'm structuring it with a Node containing the key, value, and prev/next pointers, plus a HashMap that maps keys to node indices for efficient lookups.\n\nFor the implementation, I'll maintain a Vec of Option<Node> for the doubly-linked list, with free slots managed through a stack and head/tail pointers. The `put` method will either refresh an existing key by moving it to the tail (O(1)) or, if the cache is at capacity, evict the oldest entry from the head before inserting the new one. The `get` method will be a pure lookup that doesn't affect recency. I'm also thinking through edge cases like when a refresh moves the tail itself — I need to make sure the unlinked node's pointers are set to None before re-appending it.\n\nNow I'm working through the borrow checker constraints. Since I'm using `Option<usize>` indices rather than Rc or RefCell, all pointer operations are O(1) index updates without indirection. The tricky part is that I can't hold two mutable references into `self.nodes` simultaneously, so I need to carefully sequence operations — take out the Option values, update the previous and next nodes' pointers, then update the moved node's pointers, all without holding multiple mutable borrows at once. I'm also considering whether to store values in a separate HashMap or keep them in the nodes themselves.\n\nFor the test suite, I need to write comprehensive cases: basic put/get/len, eviction count tracking, capacity handling when replacing an existing key, oldest-entry eviction on overflow, confirming that get doesn't affect recency, stress testing with many distinct keys to verify eviction counts and that only the last N keys are retained, and a panic test for zero capacity. I'll also update NOTES.md to accurately document the LRU behavior since the current notes describe an outdated bulk-clear approach that I'm replacing.\n\nNow I'm writing the implementation, starting with the core structure and the unlink/relink operations that maintain the doubly-linked list invariant.\n\nWriting unlink and relink logic... Now I'm verifying the eviction and refresh logic with concrete trace examples—checking that when we evict the oldest key or refresh an existing one, the doubly-linked list maintains correct head and tail pointers, and that the slots map stays in sync with which keys are currently cached. I'm also confirming that put operations always append to the tail, which ensures the cache correctly evicts the oldest entry when full.\n\nI'm writing a test that simulates heavy churn by cycling through keys with a large cache size, verifying that eviction counts track exactly as expected when new keys arrive at capacity, and that the cache properly replaces and refreshes entries without spurious evictions.\n\nNow I'm planning the implementation: rewrite lib.rs with a doubly-linked list and HashMap to maintain insertion order for O(1) refreshes, add comprehensive tests including churn and edge cases, update the documentation, then run cargo test locally to verify everything passes. The CodeBro memory confirms this is the right approach—bounded LRU eviction, not bulk-clearing.\n\n","time":{"start":1789579433791,"end":1789579452906}}}
