{"type":"text","timestamp":1789579768576,"sessionID":"ses_f54c41112ffemlTC4rFtd6Lmsj","part":{"id":"prt_0ab4408be001kTVlpzoQ6Y9O1q","messageID":"msg_0ab43fa5f001Qd1Wh9SGg41EL3","sessionID":"ses_f54c41112ffemlTC4rFtd6Lmsj","type":"text","text":"I'm realizing I can't use CodeBro apply because the fixture workspace isn't authorized, so I'll need to work with native file editing instead.\n\nThe user's task explicitly says to work in /tmp/opencode/phase1b/P1B-T5-ON-3/work and run cargo test directly without docker or sandbox. I'll make code edits using the native file tools and run cargo test locally.\n\nLooking at the situation: spec.md defines a bounded cache that makes room for new entries, while NOTES.md claims the cache does a bulk clear when full. The current lib.rs implements that bulk clear. CodeBro memory indicates this mass-delete approach was rejected, and the spec says to make room—so I need to replace the bulk-clear logic with something that evicts only enough entries to fit the new one.\n\nThe trap is that NOTES.md documents a stale approach, but the real fix is to implement proper LRU eviction (write-recency only, since get doesn't affect recency) while following the spec. I'll update NOTES.md to match the new behavior and keep the documentation honest.\n\nFor the O(1) LRU implementation, I'm using a HashMap to track entries plus a doubly-linked list to maintain recency order. When a put happens, I move the entry to the tail; on eviction, I pop from the head. Each eviction increments a counter.\n\nNow I'm working through the data structure details—using a Vec of Option<Node> with index-based prev/next pointers and a free list to recycle slots. On a put of an existing key, I replace the value and move that node to the tail; on a new key when full, I evict the head node and recycle its slot.\n\nI'm checking the borrow checker constraints carefully—since Node stores index-based pointers rather than references, I can safely manipulate the Vec in-place without holding conflicting mutable borrows across operations. For instance, when linking a new node to the current tail, I first set the tail's next pointer, then update the new node's prev pointer, which is fine as long as I'm not holding a mutable borrow of the tail while mutating the new node.\n\nNow I'm writing the Cache structure with the core fields for capacity tracking, the linked list of nodes, free list management, and head/tail pointers.\n\nDefining the implementation methods... I'm verifying the logic for moving nodes to the tail of the linked list, checking that when a node isn't already at the tail, I unlink it and reattach it properly. I'm also confirming the edge cases work correctly—when the cache is empty, when moving the only node, and when handling the full-cache eviction scenario where we always have at least one node to evict. Now I'm planning the test suite to cover the core behaviors: basic put/get operations, updates without eviction, LRU eviction order, the effect of get on recency, churn patterns with many keys, and the zero-capacity panic. I'm also noting that the churn test should verify the total evictions equal the number of puts minus the cache size, since once full, each new key insertion evicts exactly one entry. I'll also update the documentation to reflect that eviction follows an LRU approach rather than the stale bulk-clear strategy.\n\n","time":{"start":1789579757758,"end":1789579768574}}}
